1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,000
Come on, do it!

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:06,000
It's up!

3
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:10,000
She'll be begging for mercy when I'm done with you, Ushiwaka!

4
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:21,000
You're gonna have to come through me first if you wanna take down Ushiwaka.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:53,000
You're gonna have to come through me first if you want.

6
00:00:53,000 --> 00:01:03,420
光あれ行け 闇をかそろりして 己の道を経験に誇こう

7
00:01:03,420 --> 00:01:14,180
光あれ椅子先の絶望へ 二寸先の栄光を信じて

8
00:01:14,180 --> 00:01:25,180
光あれ椅子避ける理由はたった一つ 光あれもっと眩く俺はもうめぐ

9
00:01:25,180 --> 00:01:38,180
光あれ君の今日の輝きが 迷える友の明日を照らすよ

10
00:01:38,180 --> 00:01:48,240
光あれ椅子先より脳行き大人 目を捉えて

11
00:01:48,240 --> 00:01:51,560
光あれ Munister

12
00:02:01,500 --> 00:02:03,440
謳 unasきれた場所ね

13
00:02:03,440 --> 00:02:05,220
この天命をあった

14
00:02:05,220 --> 00:02:05,700
魔害を止めて

15
00:02:05,700 --> 00:02:07,380
首度で最初に危 baskets

16
00:02:07,380 --> 00:02:09,640
Did he just call number five a guest mobster?

17
00:02:09,640 --> 00:02:12,520
Is he gonna put a hit out on poor Hinata?

18
00:02:12,520 --> 00:02:14,120
This game's so dangerous!

19
00:02:14,120 --> 00:02:16,120
I don't think you guys heard that right.

20
00:02:17,280 --> 00:02:18,720
Send it over!

21
00:02:24,200 --> 00:02:25,040
Sorry!

22
00:02:26,500 --> 00:02:28,460
Kageyama, last for you!

23
00:02:28,460 --> 00:02:30,300
Don't let him get that point!

24
00:02:31,280 --> 00:02:34,600
Blocking isn't just the art of stopping the ball.

25
00:02:34,600 --> 00:02:37,500
Goshki, help me stop the straight.

26
00:02:44,280 --> 00:02:47,080
It's the art of smashing it down, too.

27
00:02:51,960 --> 00:02:54,760
That has got to be really disheartening for our guys.

28
00:02:54,760 --> 00:02:57,060
When you can't get around clean blocks like that,

29
00:02:57,060 --> 00:02:58,900
it can really hurt your morale.

30
00:02:59,900 --> 00:03:02,020
Feeling good!

31
00:03:02,020 --> 00:03:04,460
Get the next one!

32
00:03:05,460 --> 00:03:06,860
It's okay, Kageyama!

33
00:03:06,860 --> 00:03:09,000
If at first you don't succeed,

34
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:10,720
try and try again!

35
00:03:18,140 --> 00:03:19,680
Yeah, nice receive.

36
00:03:22,680 --> 00:03:24,100
Too easy.

37
00:03:24,100 --> 00:03:46,660
Man, it feels like I'm playing at 120% today!

38
00:03:46,660 --> 00:03:50,100
How is he able to react so quickly?

39
00:03:50,100 --> 00:03:52,100
It's not just his reaction time.

40
00:03:52,100 --> 00:03:54,640
I mean, within that mere split second,

41
00:03:54,640 --> 00:03:57,140
he analyzed three possible problems from us.

42
00:03:57,140 --> 00:04:00,340
First, since Kageyama's up front,

43
00:04:00,340 --> 00:04:01,880
we could have pursued a dump.

44
00:04:01,880 --> 00:04:04,620
So he had to guess whether or not that was going to be attempted.

45
00:04:04,620 --> 00:04:07,740
But if not, he had to guess at who was going to be our attacker.

46
00:04:07,740 --> 00:04:10,280
Was it going to be Tanaka or Hinata in the front?

47
00:04:10,280 --> 00:04:12,720
Or Asahi or Daichi in the back?

48
00:04:12,720 --> 00:04:14,920
Then assuming the ball was going to Hinata,

49
00:04:14,920 --> 00:04:15,720
which it did,

50
00:04:15,720 --> 00:04:17,760
he had to guess where he was going to aim.

51
00:04:17,760 --> 00:04:20,120
We have an advantage to see these things.

52
00:04:20,120 --> 00:04:22,200
We have a bird's eye view of the court.

53
00:04:22,200 --> 00:04:25,600
But down there, it's hard keeping tabs on everything in real time.

54
00:04:25,600 --> 00:04:28,840
Sometimes your body doesn't react as quickly as your brain.

55
00:04:28,840 --> 00:04:33,320
But with him, his body and mind are both in sync.

56
00:04:37,320 --> 00:04:40,120
Not surprised we're taking a timeout after that.

57
00:04:40,120 --> 00:04:43,480
I could have sworn that they were going to go for a back attack for sure.

58
00:04:43,480 --> 00:04:45,960
I mean, their setter even glanced back behind him.

59
00:04:45,960 --> 00:04:47,960
That was a very obvious bait.

60
00:04:47,960 --> 00:04:52,360
Huh? Karasu no setter is pretty good, I'll give him that.

61
00:04:52,360 --> 00:04:55,160
But that seemed a little too deliberate.

62
00:04:55,160 --> 00:04:59,560
Glances, footwork, and that movement.

63
00:04:59,560 --> 00:05:02,760
If Tendo picks up on any of your tells you, I don't fuck.

64
00:05:02,760 --> 00:05:04,360
Oh, I thank you.

65
00:05:04,360 --> 00:05:07,800
Sorry, bud. I'm not sharing the spotlight this game, my man.

66
00:05:07,800 --> 00:05:10,040
Hmm. It's not a big deal.

67
00:05:10,040 --> 00:05:12,920
Shit up, guys! We're taking this next point!

68
00:05:12,920 --> 00:05:15,240
No biggie, Hinata! Get us the next one!

69
00:05:15,240 --> 00:05:16,280
Will do!

70
00:05:16,280 --> 00:05:19,480
Huh? Kageyama, your face! You mad, bro?

71
00:05:19,480 --> 00:05:21,160
This is my normal face.

72
00:05:21,160 --> 00:05:21,960
Liar!

73
00:05:21,960 --> 00:05:26,200
The only thing that pisses me off more than when my own spikes get knocked down

74
00:05:26,200 --> 00:05:29,000
is when it happens to be the ones that I set up perfectly.

75
00:05:30,440 --> 00:05:34,840
How's number five able to consistently shut us out like that, coach?

76
00:05:34,840 --> 00:05:38,120
Hey, Spex, you know what a read block is, don't you?

77
00:05:38,120 --> 00:05:41,880
It's where the player reacts after he sees where the ball's being set.

78
00:05:41,880 --> 00:05:46,760
Yeah. And read in this context doesn't simply mean to read the current situation,

79
00:05:46,760 --> 00:05:50,920
but also to understand it as well. If you jump after watching the ball,

80
00:05:50,920 --> 00:05:54,920
there's little danger in getting tricked by the set. Your reaction is a little delayed,

81
00:05:54,920 --> 00:05:59,160
but if you can get a three-man block up, you'll pretty much lock out anything thrown at you.

82
00:05:59,160 --> 00:06:00,920
We're still working on this ourselves.

83
00:06:02,120 --> 00:06:06,280
Shiratori Zawa's number five is really gifted at making his reads, though.

84
00:06:06,280 --> 00:06:11,000
He's coupling his reads with his intuition, so his guest blocking is where he shines.

85
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:15,720
He's anticipating the attack before the set, based almost entirely on his instinct.

86
00:06:16,360 --> 00:06:17,240
He's psychic?

87
00:06:17,240 --> 00:06:20,440
Naturally, there will be times when his instincts are way off,

88
00:06:20,440 --> 00:06:24,040
and when that happens, it could really screw up his teammates' blocks or receives.

89
00:06:25,080 --> 00:06:29,000
To put it simply, this playstyle's results vary based on the individual,

90
00:06:29,720 --> 00:06:32,680
but I think number five's guesses are insanely accurate,

91
00:06:32,680 --> 00:06:35,240
and it appears his body is able to keep up with his mind.

92
00:06:36,200 --> 00:06:39,400
I think it's best if we assume his movements adhere to no pattern.

93
00:06:40,360 --> 00:06:42,600
Don't overthink him or let him distract you.

94
00:06:42,600 --> 00:06:43,720
Yes, sir!

95
00:06:43,720 --> 00:06:47,160
Okay, so how do you think we can prevent him from reading all of our sets?

96
00:06:47,720 --> 00:06:51,960
Maybe we should have Kageyama channel his inner Hinata and do some messy feints.

97
00:06:51,960 --> 00:06:53,480
I'd actually love to see that.

98
00:06:56,280 --> 00:06:58,280
One more pretty serve, pretty please!

99
00:07:01,480 --> 00:07:02,360
Save it up front!

100
00:07:04,040 --> 00:07:05,480
Sorry, I hit that short!

101
00:07:06,120 --> 00:07:08,760
They don't realize their feints are useless against Tendo.

102
00:07:09,400 --> 00:07:17,160
So, this is what being a well-oiled machine looks like.

103
00:07:22,120 --> 00:07:23,480
I got lost in thought.

104
00:07:23,480 --> 00:07:34,840
By the time I realized where the set was going, they already spiked it.

105
00:07:34,840 --> 00:07:39,160
You said that last one right over my head.

106
00:07:39,160 --> 00:07:41,560
That took some cojones, knowing what I'm capable of.

107
00:07:42,920 --> 00:07:45,480
You must be a pretty sore loser, huh?

108
00:07:45,480 --> 00:07:52,520
I knew you guys would be fun to play!

109
00:07:56,200 --> 00:07:57,240
It's a change ball!

110
00:07:57,240 --> 00:08:01,800
I screwed up!

111
00:08:04,120 --> 00:08:05,000
It's coming back!

112
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:05,480
On it!

113
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:07,720
Hey, I got it!

114
00:08:08,440 --> 00:08:11,560
I think it's best if we assume his movements adhere to no pattern.

115
00:08:12,280 --> 00:08:14,360
Don't overthink him or let him distract you.

116
00:08:15,160 --> 00:08:20,200
Also, with opponents like this one, it's important to use the width of the core to your advantage.

117
00:08:20,200 --> 00:08:22,200
Aren't you a quick little stinker?

118
00:08:28,840 --> 00:08:29,960
All right, that ties us!

119
00:08:29,960 --> 00:08:30,840
Nice kill!

120
00:08:30,840 --> 00:08:31,960
Oh, yeah!

121
00:08:31,960 --> 00:08:32,920
Come on, Spoon!

122
00:08:32,920 --> 00:08:33,960
That's incredible!

123
00:08:33,960 --> 00:08:35,160
Oh, yeah!

124
00:08:35,160 --> 00:08:35,960
Oh, yeah!

125
00:08:35,960 --> 00:08:41,960
There have been setters I admire for trying to get around my reads before, but that number 10's timing is insane.

126
00:08:41,960 --> 00:08:49,400
I have to keep up because if I fall even just one tempo behind, it's almost as if the ball somehow teleports beyond my reach.

127
00:08:49,960 --> 00:08:53,240
So I assume you want us to overlook your little screw-up from earlier, right?

128
00:08:54,920 --> 00:08:55,720
Guess so.

129
00:08:55,720 --> 00:08:58,040
Puns are the lowest form of comedy.

130
00:08:59,400 --> 00:09:00,600
Show us what you got!

131
00:09:06,520 --> 00:09:07,320
Nice receive!

132
00:09:07,320 --> 00:09:12,040
Nice receive!

133
00:09:12,040 --> 00:09:13,400
This is our chance to attack!

134
00:09:20,200 --> 00:09:21,240
One touchy!

135
00:09:23,800 --> 00:09:24,280
Got it!

136
00:09:24,280 --> 00:09:37,400
I can't tell if that was a feint or he changed his mind mid-play.

137
00:09:39,240 --> 00:09:44,040
Now he feels like Shira Torizawa is unpredictable on all fronts.

138
00:09:46,760 --> 00:09:51,000
I just can't figure it out. What's number five's playing style?

139
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:53,480
He wants to stop every spike all by himself.

140
00:09:54,600 --> 00:09:58,840
You can look at it this way. Tendo's blocks have been used to score points against us.

141
00:09:58,840 --> 00:10:01,720
They've been reversing all our spikes into points for their team.

142
00:10:02,600 --> 00:10:05,880
In other words, there's more to blocking than simply stopping the ball.

143
00:10:06,760 --> 00:10:11,240
If we can manage to block and maintain control, we'll be able to pull out ahead of this.

144
00:10:11,960 --> 00:10:13,880
That can lead to some points for us as well.

145
00:10:14,760 --> 00:10:21,160
So the point is, depending on how it's timed, blocking can be more valuable than spiking is in some instances.

146
00:10:21,880 --> 00:10:22,440
Interesting.

147
00:10:27,720 --> 00:10:29,320
Don't let them pull ahead.

148
00:10:29,320 --> 00:10:32,360
If they get the opportunity, they're going to leave our guys in the dust.

149
00:10:32,360 --> 00:10:46,200
It's like they hit that on purpose just to silence the haters.

150
00:10:46,200 --> 00:10:48,840
Rishiwaka is really playing on another level today.

151
00:10:50,280 --> 00:10:53,400
Blocks ultimately evolve in order to stop attacks.

152
00:10:53,960 --> 00:10:57,400
And then those attacks evolve in order to break through those new blocks.

153
00:10:57,400 --> 00:10:57,720
Huh?

154
00:10:58,600 --> 00:11:01,480
Attacks get more diverse when you throw in other elements.

155
00:11:01,480 --> 00:11:04,280
Like the back row, speed attacks, and combos.

156
00:11:04,840 --> 00:11:09,720
This leads to adapting variations in blocks, positions, and even how one jumps.

157
00:11:10,520 --> 00:11:13,480
Players who think that they are at the top of their game,

158
00:11:13,480 --> 00:11:16,680
keep finding new ways to climb higher than they thought was possible.

159
00:11:17,560 --> 00:11:19,240
Looks like you've been studying specs.

160
00:11:19,800 --> 00:11:20,840
Just observations.

161
00:11:21,400 --> 00:11:26,200
I'm sure that they will keep striving for new heights with their tenacity and fighting spirit.

162
00:11:27,400 --> 00:11:30,920
Great plays can save the team, but even more so can the power of reason.

163
00:11:35,960 --> 00:11:37,560
It's Mr. Vanilla!

164
00:11:39,880 --> 00:11:41,160
Don't let him get under your skin.

165
00:11:44,200 --> 00:11:45,400
Serve it up, Tai Chi!

166
00:11:47,400 --> 00:11:48,200
That's gonna fall!

167
00:11:49,080 --> 00:11:50,840
That's a chance ball! Chance ball!

168
00:11:50,840 --> 00:11:51,400
Damn it!

169
00:11:51,400 --> 00:11:52,200
Sorry, guys!

170
00:11:54,120 --> 00:11:55,800
Is this going to be a lag attack?

171
00:11:57,400 --> 00:12:00,680
Uh-oh!

172
00:12:05,960 --> 00:12:07,880
Hello. I'm Mr. Vanilla.

173
00:12:08,520 --> 00:12:10,120
Your timed lag attack.

174
00:12:10,120 --> 00:12:14,280
It might have been able to trip up our feral child, but it won't work on me.

175
00:12:14,280 --> 00:12:15,400
Continue to use it, though.

176
00:12:15,960 --> 00:12:18,920
I thought Karasuno's first years were just annoying little punks,

177
00:12:18,920 --> 00:12:21,240
but you really are a cut above the rest.

178
00:12:21,240 --> 00:12:22,040
Why, thank you.

179
00:12:22,040 --> 00:12:24,200
You're pissing me off!

180
00:12:25,640 --> 00:12:27,480
Serve it up, Asahi!

181
00:12:27,480 --> 00:12:46,120
Please keep a level head.

182
00:12:47,720 --> 00:12:49,560
You're Karasuno's voice of reason.

183
00:12:49,560 --> 00:13:17,080
We'll be fine.

184
00:13:17,080 --> 00:13:19,880
We're definitely going to get our chance.

185
00:13:25,640 --> 00:13:28,600
Feels like Yamaguchi is staring a hole right through my head.

186
00:13:28,600 --> 00:13:34,280
One touch!

187
00:13:34,280 --> 00:13:36,280
I got it!

188
00:13:47,080 --> 00:13:47,880
One touch!

189
00:13:47,880 --> 00:13:49,160
This guy is bugging me.

190
00:13:49,160 --> 00:13:57,720
I got it!

191
00:13:57,720 --> 00:13:58,680
I got it!

192
00:13:58,680 --> 00:14:01,880
That one's for you, Koski, you idiot!

193
00:14:01,880 --> 00:14:03,560
Don't just stare at the ball!

194
00:14:03,560 --> 00:14:04,440
Do something!

195
00:14:08,440 --> 00:14:09,480
Uh, yes, sir.

196
00:14:10,200 --> 00:14:11,720
Oh, Koski.

197
00:14:12,280 --> 00:14:17,240
Here I thought the infamous demon, Koj, might actually be chilling out in his old age.

198
00:14:18,120 --> 00:14:19,000
Go get him!

199
00:14:26,600 --> 00:14:28,040
Suga, let's do this!

200
00:14:29,320 --> 00:14:30,440
In the front ray on!

201
00:14:30,440 --> 00:14:40,120
It's time for a synchronized attack!

202
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:45,960
Yeah!

203
00:14:45,960 --> 00:14:48,120
Nice one, Kaviyama!

204
00:14:48,120 --> 00:14:49,240
What was that?

205
00:14:49,240 --> 00:14:51,000
Am I going blind and senile?

206
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:52,680
Did the center just make that?

207
00:14:53,480 --> 00:14:54,840
No, the arrogance!

208
00:14:55,320 --> 00:14:57,880
Guess being disrespectful is a family trait!

209
00:14:57,880 --> 00:14:59,160
Oh, that's right.

210
00:14:59,160 --> 00:15:02,360
Karasuno's head coach is Coach Yukai's grandson, isn't he?

211
00:15:02,360 --> 00:15:03,080
That's right.

212
00:15:03,640 --> 00:15:07,720
That imbecile Yukai always relied too much on gimmicks to make up for weaknesses.

213
00:15:08,440 --> 00:15:10,440
That old geriatric bastard!

214
00:15:10,440 --> 00:15:15,400
It's archaic to rely only on a single player to carry your team.

215
00:15:15,400 --> 00:15:20,360
And you're totally reckless by sending the entire team to attack so no one's left to follow up!

216
00:15:21,560 --> 00:15:24,520
Sadly, because the high school roster changes every year,

217
00:15:25,160 --> 00:15:26,920
we're limited to what we can achieve.

218
00:15:27,480 --> 00:15:30,600
Polishing what raw materials we have is our best bet.

219
00:15:33,720 --> 00:15:34,440
Let's go!

220
00:15:34,440 --> 00:15:36,280
It's how we'll find success.

221
00:15:36,280 --> 00:15:40,600
Man, that old coach Washito hasn't changed a bit, has he?

222
00:15:41,400 --> 00:15:43,240
His motto's simple as best.

223
00:15:43,240 --> 00:15:45,000
He's always liked height and power.

224
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:49,560
In fact, he'd recruit anyone who met those standards, whether they'd ever played or not.

225
00:15:50,520 --> 00:15:54,040
And regardless if there was even any room for them on the squad,

226
00:15:54,040 --> 00:15:57,480
as long as they fit his definition of a powerhouse, they'd make the team.

227
00:15:58,200 --> 00:16:02,680
He would work closely with his new recruits and put them through a grueling spartan-like training.

228
00:16:03,240 --> 00:16:07,320
I see. So can the method he uses always produce an all-star player?

229
00:16:07,320 --> 00:16:13,080
Of course not. Finding a player with Ushiwaka's height, power, and level of skill is just like hitting the lottery.

230
00:16:13,640 --> 00:16:18,120
But when Washito finds someone like him, he's going to make him the axis of the team,

231
00:16:18,120 --> 00:16:20,040
and make sure everyone knows their role.

232
00:16:20,040 --> 00:16:25,240
That's just Shiratorizawa's style.

233
00:16:27,000 --> 00:16:29,960
Man, Ushiwaka just will not let up!

234
00:16:31,400 --> 00:16:35,960
This is definitely going to be an uphill battle, but we can still turn the tide.

235
00:16:37,960 --> 00:16:38,840
Nice receive!

236
00:16:42,280 --> 00:16:43,160
Keep it up, guys!

237
00:16:43,160 --> 00:16:44,280
Nice kill, Boshki!

238
00:16:45,080 --> 00:16:46,120
It's all you, Shirabu!

239
00:16:50,040 --> 00:16:52,280
I have it! For you!

240
00:16:58,120 --> 00:16:59,720
Whoa! Wait it down!

241
00:17:00,600 --> 00:17:02,840
I'm so nervous, I'm having palpitations!

242
00:17:02,840 --> 00:17:04,280
I think I am too.

243
00:17:10,440 --> 00:17:12,920
With me around, you can beat anyone!

244
00:17:12,920 --> 00:17:22,280
I can't bring myself to say confident things like Hinata.

245
00:17:23,080 --> 00:17:25,160
I can, however, lend you my strength.

246
00:17:25,720 --> 00:17:26,360
It's all you!

247
00:17:37,400 --> 00:17:38,280
They're breaking up!

248
00:17:38,280 --> 00:17:38,840
Yes!

249
00:17:40,280 --> 00:17:40,840
This is it.

250
00:17:41,960 --> 00:17:43,560
